Below is a **complete, beginner-friendly, end-to-end CI setup** using:

* Simple Java app
* GitHub repository
* AWS EC2 (Ubuntu)
* Jenkins
* Maven
* GitHub Webhook (automatic build on code change)

I’ve written this so you can **directly follow it for your DevOps lab / project demo**.

---

# PART 1 – Create Simple Java + Maven Application

## 1. Create project on your local machine

```bash
mkdir java-ci-project
cd java-ci-project
```

Create folders:

```bash
mkdir -p src/main/java
```

---

## 2. Create Java file

**src/main/java/Hello.java**

```java
public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("CI/CD Pipeline Working Successfully!");
    }
}
```

---

## 3. Create Maven pom.xml

**pom.xml**

```xml
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0
         http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>com.devops</groupId>
    <artifactId>java-ci-project</artifactId>
    <version>1.0</version>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.8.1</version>
                <configuration>
                    <source>8</source>
                    <target>8</target>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
```

---

## 4. Test locally

```bash
mvn clean package
```

You should see **BUILD SUCCESS**.

---

# PART 2 – Push Code to GitHub

## 1. Create GitHub repository

Name: `java-ci-project`

---

## 2. Push code

```bash
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial Java CI project"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/<your-username>/java-ci-project.git
git push -u origin main
```

---

# PART 3 – Launch EC2 Instance

1. AWS Console → EC2 → Launch Instance
2. Ubuntu 22.04
3. Instance type: t2.micro
4. Allow ports:

| Port | Purpose |
| ---- | ------- |
| 22   | SSH     |
| 8080 | Jenkins |

5. Download key pair

---

# PART 4 – Install Java, Maven, Jenkins on EC2

SSH into EC2:

```bash
ssh -i key.pem ubuntu@<EC2-Public-IP>
```

---

## 1. Install Java

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install openjdk-17-jdk -y
java -version
```

---

## 2. Install Maven

```bash
sudo apt install maven -y
mvn -version
```

---

## 3. Install Jenkins

```bash
curl -fsSL https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian/jenkins.io-2023.key | sudo tee \
  /usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc > /dev/null

echo deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc] \
  https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian binary/ | sudo tee \
  /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null

sudo apt update
sudo apt install jenkins -y
```

Start Jenkins:

```bash
sudo systemctl start jenkins
sudo systemctl enable jenkins
```

---

## 4. Unlock Jenkins

```bash
sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword
```

Open:

```
http://EC2-PUBLIC-IP:8080
```

Install **Suggested Plugins** → Create admin user.

---

# PART 5 – Create Jenkins Job

## 1. New Item → Freestyle Project

Name: `java-ci-pipeline`

---

## 2. Configure GitHub Repo

Source Code Management → Git

Repo URL:

```
https://github.com/<your-username>/java-ci-project.git
```

Branch:

```
main
```

---

## 3. Configure Maven Build

Build Steps → Add → Invoke top-level Maven targets

Goals:

```
clean package
```

---

# PART 6 – Enable Auto Build (Webhook)

## Jenkins side

Job → Configure → Build Triggers:

✔ GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling

Save.

---

## GitHub side

Repository → Settings → Webhooks → Add webhook

Payload URL:

```
http://EC2-PUBLIC-IP:8080/github-webhook/
```

Content type:

```
application/json
```

Select:

✔ Just the push event

Add webhook.

---

# PART 7 – Test Auto Build

Make any code change:

```java
System.out.println("Auto build working!");
```

Then:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Trigger build"
git push
```

---

## Result

✔ GitHub push triggers webhook
✔ Jenkins automatically pulls code
✔ Maven builds project
✔ Build success shown in Jenkins console

